Z||000019||GarethWright||09-25-2001||04:24 PM||||Once ya get in game, start pricing up engine mods, if you buy Ford (but why? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) you probably have a 2-spd Auto transmission, this isn't Too bad, as you don't really have the HPs right now to run any better gearing.  First mods are get an Aluminum Intake Manifold (lighter is better is more expensive in mose cases) the Edelbrock Tunnel Ram is a good one, next is the Exhaust Manifold, get the best you can afford.  If you still have money left over get a hood scoop (mine with 4% torque boost gets me an extra 21 horses).  Start looking at transmissions when you want to  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>For more money while you're doing this, run Sponsored Trials to get used to game physics (don't have to pay to fix sponsored cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) then Open Trials with your own car, the Gearborn Tri-Oval and the Fairground Big-O are good easy starter tracks.  The better your time the more money you get, and the more points to the next level.  (your level effects how big your paycheck is every Friday, with Bonuses when you get ranks)<BR>Once you decide to go Drag Racing, the first thing you want to do is figure your 1/4 mile time, so make or join a drag race with no handicap, no restrictions and no cash.  Then hit the gas on the 2nd Orange light (race time starts when you cross the start line, not when you hit the gas) but be careful not to beat the green light, as this will forfeit this round for you.  Write down your Elapsed Time from the screen after each round, the best time you run is what you want to use in your Dial-in to handicap handicapped drags.<BR>Happy Motoring and see you in there hopefully!<BR>-Gareth||||reg||1
